== Biography ==

Hugh Glasgow is named as a father in four Kilmarnock, Ayrshire, parish baptism entries between 1732 and 1744:

* [[Glasgow-4142|Mary Glasgow]], baptised 17 December 1732, with mother [[McGechan-24|Margaret McGechan]];<ref name="Mary1732">Findmypast, ''Scotland, Parish Births & Baptisms 1564–1929'', Kilmarnock Old Parish Register, [https://www.findmypast.co.uk/transcript?id=SCOT%2FOPR%2FBAP%2F3599128&tab=this Mary Glasgow, 17 December 1732].</ref>
* [[Glasgow-4115|Margaret Glasgow]], baptised 31 August 1738, with mother Margaret McGechan;<ref name="Margaret1738">Findmypast, ''Scotland, Parish Births & Baptisms 1564–1929'', Kilmarnock Old Parish Register; full index citation on [[Glasgow-4115]].</ref>
* [[Glasgow-4104|Hugh Glasgow]], baptised 29 August 1742, with mother Margaret McGechan;<ref name="Hugh1742">Findmypast, ''Scotland, Parish Births & Baptisms 1564–1929'', Kilmarnock Old Parish Register; full index citation on [[Glasgow-4104]].</ref>
* [[Glasgow-4108|Mary Glasgow]], baptised 11 April 1744, with mother [[Giffen-664|Margaret Giffen]].<ref name="Mary1744">Findmypast, ''Scotland, Parish Births & Baptisms 1564–1929'', Kilmarnock Old Parish Register, [https://www.findmypast.co.uk/transcript?id=SCOT%2FOPR%2FBAP%2F3600620&tab=this Mary Glasgow, 11 April 1744].</ref>

No birth, baptism or death record for this adult Hugh has been identified. Birth before about 1713 is an adult-age estimate from the first child; Kilmarnock is his recorded household parish, not a proved birthplace.

== Research Notes ==

The former Glasgow-4167 profile represented the Hugh named with Margaret Giffen in 1744. It was merged into this Kilmarnock Hugh profile on 27 September 2026. The same father name and parish across an otherwise continuous series of baptisms, with no distinct same-name adult identified, support one identity. The mothers' surnames differ. They may represent two women, a remarriage, or a register/transcription discrepancy; both named mothers and all four child entries are preserved. No death or remarriage is asserted without a record.

The two Mary baptisms of 1732 and 1744 are separate entries. They may concern successive daughters of the same name if the first died young, or distinct families if the father identification proves wrong. Compare the original entries and search Kilmarnock marriages and burials before resolving that child-level question.

